FEATURE: modal-stacking
DATE: 2026-08-03
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-displayer-verification'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — New "Stacking" section
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(no such section — the guide described a single modal at a time)

NEW_SECTION
## Stacking

Modals stack. A trigger placed **inside** a displayed modal opens a new modal on top
of it; the one underneath stays open and intact, and closing the top one reveals it
again.
[...markup example, per-layer overlay/z-index/scroll explanation, LIFO rule,
closeAll(), page-state-at-the-stack-boundary explanation, shared-stack-across-
instances explanation, and the two refused cases...]

REASON
Documents the feature added by this change. The section covers the parts a consumer
cannot infer from the API: that layers close strictly top down, that the body freeze
and scroll capture happen at the stack boundary rather than per modal, that the
stack is shared across every ModalDisplayer instance on the page, and that opening a
modal onto itself or onto an already displayed modal is refused rather than allowed
to corrupt state.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — Replace the "one modal at a time" limitation
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
- **One modal at a time.** Opening a second modal while one is open is not supported; close the first from a trigger inside it.
- **`Escape` always closes**, independent of `exitOnAway`.

NEW_SECTION
- **`Escape` always closes** the topmost layer, independent of `exitOnAway`.

REASON
The stated limitation no longer holds and its advice was actively harmful: "close the
first from a trigger inside it" describes the nesting path that silently destroyed
the first modal and left the page permanently frozen. It is removed outright; see
CHANGE 6 for why no replacement caveat belongs here.

================================================================================
CHANGE 6 — State last-in-first-out as the stack's defining property, not a caveat
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
Each layer gets its own overlay element with an increasing `z-index`, its own responsive width, and its own scroll container, so a tall modal at layer 2 scrolls independently of layer 1. Layers are strictly last-in-first-out: `Escape`, an overlay click and a close button all pop **only the topmost layer**. Use `closeAll()` to unwind the whole stack at once.

(and, under "Requirements and gotchas")
- **Layers close top-down only.** There is no API to close a layer from the middle of the stack; that would leave the visual ordering inconsistent.

NEW_SECTION
Each layer gets its own overlay element with an increasing `z-index`, its own responsive width, and its own scroll container, so a tall modal at layer 2 scrolls independently of layer 1.

**Layers close last-in-first-out, and that is the point of a modal stack rather than a restriction on it.** A modal takes exclusive control of interaction: while it is up, everything beneath it is unreachable by definition. So a covered layer has no way to be acted on, and closing one out of order would leave the dialog it spawned floating above nothing. `Escape`, an overlay click and a close button therefore all pop **only the topmost layer**, and there is deliberately no API that takes a specific layer. `closeAll()` unwinds the whole stack when you need to dismiss everything at once — on a route change or logout, for example.

(the "Requirements and gotchas" bullet is deleted)

REASON
Corrects a framing error, on reviewer feedback, with no code change.

The first write-up listed top-down unwinding under "Requirements and gotchas" and,
in the summary, under "Known limitations" — as though closing a middle layer were a
capability the implementation had failed to provide. That is the wrong way round.
Last-in-first-out is not a constraint imposed on a modal stack; it is what makes it
a modal stack. A modal is modal precisely because it takes exclusive control of
interaction, so every layer below the top is unreachable by construction and has no
user-reachable path to being dismissed. An API that closed a specific layer would
let code desynchronise the visual stack from the interaction model, leaving a child
dialog floating above a parent that no longer exists.

Filing an invariant as a shortcoming also invites the wrong follow-up work: a future
reader would reasonably treat it as a gap worth closing. The text now states the
rule positively, explains why it follows from modality, and notes that the absence
of a layer-targeting API is deliberate rather than pending.

The implementation already enforced this and is unchanged: close(), Escape, away
clicks and close buttons all route through closeTopLayer(), and no exported member
accepts a layer. Only the documentation was wrong.

================================================================================
CHANGE 3 — API table: closeAll(), depth, and top-of-stack semantics
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
| `initialize()` | method | Links styles, builds the overlay, attaches listeners. Call once. |
| `close()` | method | Closes the open modal programmatically; no-op when nothing is open. |
| `mainForm` | getter | `{ element, id }` for the *original* content element, or `null` when closed. |
| `displayForm` | getter | `{ element, id }` for the *displayed* clone in the overlay, or `null` when closed. |

NEW_SECTION
| `initialize()` | method | Links styles and attaches listeners. Call once. |
| `close()` | method | Closes the topmost open modal; no-op when nothing is open. |
| `closeAll()` | method | Unwinds every open layer immediately, without animation. |
| `mainForm` | getter | `{ element, id }` for the *original* content element of the topmost modal, or `null` when none is open. |
| `displayForm` | getter | `{ element, id }` for the *displayed* clone of the topmost modal, or `null` when none is open. |
| `depth` | getter | Number of modals currently stacked (`0` when none is open). |

REASON
Adds the two new members and restates the existing ones in terms of the stack. The
initialize() description also drops "builds the overlay", which is no longer true —
overlays are created per layer when a modal opens, so no overlay exists in the
document until then.

================================================================================
CHANGE 4 — New "Internal DOM" section, and the getElementById note
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(not documented)

NEW_SECTION
## Internal DOM
[...markup of one overlay per layer, and the note that the first layer keeps
data-id="vModalStyles" and id="newModal" for backward compatibility...]

- While a modal is open, `document.getElementById(<modal id>)` returns the **displayed copy**, not the hidden original ...

REASON
Two things consumers need and could previously only discover by reading the source.
The DOM section matters because writing CSS against a modal requires knowing the
wrapper structure, and because anyone with existing selectors on #newModal needs to
know it now exists only on the first layer and that .vModalHost is the forward
compatible target.

The getElementById note documents long standing behaviour that was never written
down: the displayed copy carries the modal's id while the source gives it up. It
becomes materially more important with stacking, since it is the reason a trigger
pointing at an already displayed modal is ignored.

================================================================================
CHANGE 5 — Width guidance in requirements
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
- **Give the content an explicit width and background.** The overlay provides no chrome of its own.

NEW_SECTION
- **Give the content an explicit background.** The overlay provides no chrome of its own. Width comes from the responsive widths above, so the content is sized for you.

REASON
Follows the behaviour change recorded as CHANGE 10 in the module log. Responsive
widths are now applied on open for every effect, including "none", so telling the
consumer to set an explicit width on the content would conflict with the width the
component itself applies.
